// ERROR-ZYB-003 — `@ x:coleccion` in a file body is not an ambiguous lifetime.
//
// Every one of these used to produce
//
//     warning: ambiguous lifetime for 'c'
//       = note: variable is modified inside a loop
//       = help: consider using explicit lifetime annotation
//
// about a variable nothing modifies, in the most ordinary construct the
// language has. It fired only in a file body — not inside a function, not
// inside a module — which is why the six earlier LDV projects never saw one:
// their code lives in functions and modules, and what goes in a file body is
// scripts and test suites, which is what ZyBank had.
//
// The cause was not a bad heuristic. A loop iterator is excluded from the
// automatic destruction schedule because the loop owns its lifetime, and that
// exclusion was implemented by setting the SAME flag the analyzer reports to
// the programmer. One flag, two jobs. They are separate now: `loop_bound`
// excludes it from destruction and says nothing to anyone.
//
// The help made it worse — it suggested an explicit lifetime annotation, and
// the form that would need (`@ c°:l`) does not parse. The only thing that
// silenced the warning was renaming `c` to `_c`, the prefix for *unused*:
// lying in the code to quiet a warning that was itself a lie.
//
// This file's golden is its `check` output, and it must stay empty.
l = ["a", "b"]
@ c:l {
    >> c ¶
}

@ i:1..3 {
    >> i ¶
}

@ x:["z"] {
    >> x ¶
}

d = (k: 1)
@ par:d {
    >> par ¶
}

nums() { <~ [7, 8] }
@ n:nums() {
    >> n ¶
}

// A real ambiguous lifetime still warns — the finding this analyzer exists for.
// It lives next door in bucle_con_aviso_real.zy: mixing the two here would make
// this golden unable to say which of them it was watching. Both files are
// needed, and the pair is the point — the first fix for this went too far and
// silenced the real one too, which the analyzer's own unit test caught.
